00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:11.870
how Anthos will help in
this particular situation.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:17.380
APARNA SINHA: Yeah,
so Anthos is not

00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:21.610
a platform that needs
to be in Google Cloud.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:25.060
So, for example, when
we talked to HSBC,

00:25:25.060 --> 00:25:27.365
they are running in regions
where Google Cloud is not

00:25:27.365 --> 00:25:27.865
available.

00:25:27.865 --> 00:25:29.198
AUDIENCE: No, I understand that.

00:25:29.198 --> 00:25:34.330
But I want to understand how
data warehousing application--

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:38.020
because the word, application,
to me, the way it's being used

00:25:38.020 --> 00:25:40.090
is user front-end applications.

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:41.480
I can see that.

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:44.440
But when you have an
ecosystem of components

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:47.140
that forms a data
warehouse application,

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:50.620
how is that planed
to be migrated?

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:53.140
APARNA SINHA: So
it's not necessarily

00:25:53.140 --> 00:25:54.490
meant to be migrated.

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:56.320
It sort of depends on
whether the customer

00:25:56.320 --> 00:25:58.930
wishes to migrate
their data warehouse,

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:02.200
including all parts
of the data warehouse.

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:04.015
So I'm not sure
if I'm necessarily

00:26:04.015 --> 00:26:05.710
answering your question.

00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:09.460
But the example that I would
give of a regulated company,

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:14.155
which is HSBC, is that they're
running in environments where

00:26:14.155 --> 00:26:15.280
there's no cloud available.

00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:17.950
For example, they're
in Saudi Arabia.

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:19.750
China is similar.

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:23.650
And they have data that
they need to keep on-prem

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:24.820
for regulatory reasons.

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:27.050
They're not moving to the cloud.

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:30.505
And so the benefit of
actually the on-prem component

00:26:30.505 --> 00:26:33.190
of Anthos, which is GKE
On-Prem, the reason that they're

00:26:33.190 --> 00:26:36.730
using it is because it gives
them a consistent environment.

00:26:36.730 --> 00:26:40.630
It's not in the cloud, but it's
an environment that's very--

00:26:40.630 --> 00:26:42.820
that's why we say,
bringing the cloud to you.

00:26:42.820 --> 00:26:46.460
Because we're bringing the cloud
to your on-prem environment.

00:26:46.460 --> 00:26:49.400
And you run it the same way that
you would run it in the cloud.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:56.100
Your data warehouse may or may
not be running in Kubernetes.

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:58.630
Your data warehouse may
be running outside, right?

00:26:58.630 --> 00:26:59.790
It's running outside.

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:02.790
But it can still
use the capabilities

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:06.900
of service management
and of policy management

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:11.640
as long as you can expose your
application, your various parts

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:14.380
of your data warehouse
application and services.

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:15.600
And so the niche case--

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:17.308
Mani talked about this
in the spotlight--

00:27:17.308 --> 00:27:20.400
he said that he wants
everything in his environment

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:22.110
to be a service.

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:25.160
He wants the data that is
on-prem to be front-ended

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:28.392
by a service that can be
accessed through a set of APIs.

00:27:28.392 --> 00:27:29.850
And that is something
that he wants

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:33.480
to manage as a
standard in his on-prem

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:35.520
as well as in the cloud.

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:40.080
So that's the way, at least
in the Anthos philosophy,

00:27:40.080 --> 00:27:41.910
that we would manage
an on-prem use.

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:46.460
ALBERTO MAESTRO:
I think understood

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:50.240
a nuance of the question,
which I hinted it before.

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:53.660
Before Anthos, it used to be
called Cloud Services Platform.

00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:58.230
And the actual name
means that we wanted

00:27:58.230 --> 00:27:59.880
it's a platform for services.

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:01.580
And we are working
with our customers,

00:28:01.580 --> 00:28:03.140
and we are listening to you.

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:06.350
And we are going
to bring whatever

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:09.020
services that we need to
bring to Anthos, whether it's

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:11.120
first party or third
party, to make sure

00:28:11.120 --> 00:28:13.070
that it addresses your needs.

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:16.730
Today, we are setting
up just the foundation

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:18.240
of that platform.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:22.790
But we are working very
closely with our customers

00:28:22.790 --> 00:28:26.930
to understand, not just that
you need the capabilities to run

00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:28.500
your applications
on the platform,

00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:33.470
but what services do you need
to be available on the platform?

00:28:33.470 --> 00:28:36.170
I'm excited, because our
third-party partners can

00:28:36.170 --> 00:28:38.915
actually get there very fast.

00:28:38.915 --> 00:28:41.540
We're working with them to bring
a lot of those services there.

00:28:46.535 --> 00:28:48.410
Of course, we are working
with our customers,

00:28:48.410 --> 00:28:50.990
and figuring out, is their
first-party services that you

00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:52.980
want to see in that platform?

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:56.220
And having that conversation to
make sure that at some point,

00:28:56.220 --> 00:28:57.858
you have all that you need.

00:28:57.858 --> 00:28:59.400
CHEN GOLDBERG: And
probably something

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:01.620
that you've also
been thinking about,

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:03.910
you don't want to containerize
or migrate everything

00:29:03.910 --> 00:29:04.410
like that.

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:06.310
You can actually move
to a managed product.

00:29:06.310 --> 00:29:08.970
So we have our own data
warehouse services.

00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:12.300
We've just acquired Alooma,
which also helps migrating.

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:14.760
AUDIENCE: It's not
available in China.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:15.510
CHEN GOLDBERG: OK.

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:18.930
So I'm just saying that
that's the things that--

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:21.755
on top of that, we are also
moving services to the cloud.

00:29:21.755 --> 00:29:23.130
RAE WANG: So I'm
actually working

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:25.380
with a number of customers
who need to run potentially

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:27.690
on other public
clouds in environments

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:29.810
where Google doesn't
have a data center.

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:30.600
And when we talked
to them about,

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:32.250
how do you make your
workloads portable

00:29:32.250 --> 00:29:33.870
between multiple clouds?

00:29:33.870 --> 00:29:37.100
The only obvious
solution is going with--

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:39.540
whether it's in compute, or
storage, in other places--

00:29:39.540 --> 00:29:41.472
going with open
source standards.

00:29:41.472 --> 00:29:43.930
So they're using open source
standards for their workloads,

00:29:43.930 --> 00:29:46.230
and then using a
managed control plane.

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:48.040
And as Alberto said,
we're starting,

00:29:48.040 --> 00:29:49.950
I think, from a compute
workloads and API

00:29:49.950 --> 00:29:52.278
