00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:09.840
will be able to do all these
configurations I already

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:12.410
configure in this environment
and help me to move it.

00:32:12.410 --> 00:32:15.420
APARNA SINHA: Yeah,
so definitely.

00:32:15.420 --> 00:32:18.840
Actually, I would say that
the challenge of migrating

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:23.010
from a VM to GKE is
a bigger challenge

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:27.760
than moving from OpenShift
to Anthos or GKE.

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:30.690
It is actually much
easier, because you already

00:32:30.690 --> 00:32:33.450
have a set of YAML,
and there's only

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.490
a couple of objects in
OpenShift that are different.

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:37.860
There's a different CLI.

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:40.080
You can, essentially,
do the migration

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:41.750
without any sort of tooling.

00:32:41.750 --> 00:32:44.650
It's not that complicated.

00:32:44.650 --> 00:32:47.040
Chen mentioned our
multi-cluster capability--

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:49.470
our multi-cluster, multi-cloud
registration capability.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:54.510
So currently, that is for a
number of different conformant

00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:55.680
distributions of Kubernetes.

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:58.750
I think we will work to
bring OpenShift into that.

00:32:58.750 --> 00:33:03.090
So you can have an OpenShift
cluster, and a GKE On-Prem

00:33:03.090 --> 00:33:06.240
cluster, and a
kops cluster in AWS

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:10.410
or wherever it is that you want
and view all of your workloads

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:14.340
and all of your resources
from a single place

00:33:14.340 --> 00:33:17.350
and also to deploy workloads
across those clusters.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:20.610
So in that sense,
we'd like users

00:33:20.610 --> 00:33:23.280
to be able to manage
their Kubernetes

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:24.360
environments the same.

00:33:24.360 --> 00:33:27.690
If there's a migration
from OpenShift to GKE,

00:33:27.690 --> 00:33:30.300
I don't think that
that's complicated.

00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:33.840
But perhaps, your question was
about the developer experience,

00:33:33.840 --> 00:33:36.420
because there is a
pass, essentially,

00:33:36.420 --> 00:33:39.330
right, that's built
on top of Kubernetes,

00:33:39.330 --> 00:33:41.010
in the case of OpenShift.

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:43.810
And Anthos is a lot more
modular in that sense.

00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:47.580
So there is the
cluster management,

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:49.560
multi-cluster management layer.

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:52.470
There is also an
additional, what we call,

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:55.740
module, which has the
developer experience.

00:33:55.740 --> 00:34:00.300
And that developer experience
is essentially Cloud Run.

00:34:00.300 --> 00:34:01.810
I don't know if you've tried it.

00:34:01.810 --> 00:34:08.130
It's extremely simple, and it
is based underneath on Knative.

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:10.679
It's a hosted
offering of Knative,

00:34:10.679 --> 00:34:14.250
and so it has automated
serving capabilities

00:34:14.250 --> 00:34:19.170
that, for a developer, you can
essentially go from your source

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:21.989
code or a container
in a registry

00:34:21.989 --> 00:34:24.429
and within less than a minute.

00:34:24.429 --> 00:34:28.139
So in seconds, you have an
externally exposed endpoint

00:34:28.139 --> 00:34:30.750
that is your application.

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:33.207
And it is automatically scaled.

00:34:33.207 --> 00:34:35.040
You don't have to know
what a deployment is.

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:36.480
You don't have to know
what a service is.

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:38.460
You don't have to set
up any kind of ingress.

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:40.770
You don't even have to
know about the cluster.

00:34:40.770 --> 00:34:44.790
That can all be done for
you by some central admin.

00:34:44.790 --> 00:34:46.150
So with Cloud Run--

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:50.790
and that's really, I think,
it's a pass essentially.

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:54.870
With Cloud Run, you
get all of that set up.

00:34:54.870 --> 00:34:59.160
And as you get more traffic,
the system automatically

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:05.290
scales the pods, scales the
cluster both up and down.

00:35:05.290 --> 00:35:10.078
So all of that is what is the
Anthos developer experience.

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:16.330
AUDIENCE: So my question
is probably a little bit

00:35:16.330 --> 00:35:19.960
confusing, and that's where
the ethos of the question is.

00:35:19.960 --> 00:35:22.510
Let me try to give a story, and
I'll try to bring it together

00:35:22.510 --> 00:35:23.590
in the question.

00:35:23.590 --> 00:35:27.580
So we, in middle
management, have

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:30.460
to work with the engineering
teams, the developers.

00:35:30.460 --> 00:35:33.070
And you answered a
part of my question

00:35:33.070 --> 00:35:35.080
over there with Cloud Run--

00:35:35.080 --> 00:35:38.580
how it can be write
once and used many times

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:41.740
or write one and run many times.

00:35:41.740 --> 00:35:46.360
But then we have to make a
case to senior management

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:50.470
or the finance team when they're
actually signing the bill,

00:35:50.470 --> 00:35:53.920
what's the value prop
with all these services?

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:56.510
What's the value
prop with Anthos?

00:35:56.510 --> 00:36:01.450
Why should we go
from just a Compute

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:05.730
Engine, where we are not running
on workloads in the cloud?

00:36:05.730 --> 00:36:08.380
We did convince them
about Kubernetes.

00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:13.450
We did convince them
about a managed offering.

00:36:13.450 --> 00:36:17.470
But then what's the value
prop with all these services?

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:21.490
And what is it going to
cost us versus having

00:36:21.490 --> 00:36:27.580
our own developers spend some
time building and managing

00:36:27.580 --> 00:36:29.230
things on top of compute?

00:36:29.230 --> 00:36:33.340
Because I'll borrow from
what my friend said,

00:36:33.340 --> 00:36:36.400
we are still a legacy company.

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:39.770
Cloud development has a
lot of philosophy, right?

00:36:39.770 --> 00:36:43.660
Even before we can keep up
with it, new abilities come up,

00:36:43.660 --> 00:36:45.540
new facilities come up.

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:48.910
There are new marketplaces
which come up.

00:36:48.910 --> 00:36:52.210
So my point is,
while a part of it

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:56.170
was already answered with
Cloud Run, what's the value

00:36:56.170 --> 00:37:00.790
prop in these services
that we can imbibe them

00:37:00.790 --> 00:37:04.270
in our work flow, or we can
think about moving to them

00:37:04.270 --> 00:37:05.430
in the future?

00:37:05.430 --> 00:37:06.540
So does that help?

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:10.880
I'm sorry if it's confusing,
but that's, as I said,

00:37:10.880 --> 00:37:13.300
it's the ethos of where we
are in middle management.

00:37:13.300 --> 00:37:14.200
APARNA SINHA: I
think several of us

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:15.783
probably want to
answer this question.

00:37:15.783 --> 00:37:18.610
Let me just give a brief answer.

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:23.380
From the CIO's perspective,
the top line about Anthos

00:37:23.380 --> 00:37:28.810
is that it minimizes or
removes the risk of lock-in,

00:37:28.810 --> 00:37:32.050
and it maximizes your choice.

00:37:32.050 --> 00:37:35.170
It gives you the choice
to deploy anywhere.

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:37.060
And that's great.

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:38.665
When you bring it
down, the piece

00:37:38.665 --> 00:37:40.540
that you don't have--
so you mentioned you've

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:42.430
got the environment
running already.

00:37:42.430 --> 00:37:43.753
The piece that you don't have--

00:37:43.753 --> 00:37:45.670
there's several pieces--
but one of the pieces

00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:47.650
is, you don't have a
multi-cluster management

00:37:47.650 --> 00:37:48.820
capability.

00:37:48.820 --> 00:37:51.370
That's something that's
unique in Anthos,

00:37:51.370 --> 00:37:54.670
and it spans multiple clouds
for workload management.

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:57.790
You don't really have the
centralized policy and security

00:37:57.790 --> 00:37:58.360
control.

00:37:58.360 --> 00:38:01.000
And that's extremely
important for enterprises.

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:03.100
And then, of course, that
separation of concerns

00:38:03.100 --> 00:38:06.060
between developers-- giving
them an easy workflow,

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:07.810
where they don't have
to know the details,

00:38:07.810 --> 00:38:10.690
and then giving
the operators all

00:38:10.690 --> 00:38:15.440
of the power to control things
at scale in a standard way.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:18.700
So I think that's what
I would say to the CIO.

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:21.130
Of course, it
comes down to, what

00:38:21.130 --> 00:38:25.780
is the TCO of doing it this way
versus doing it sort of ad-hoc,

00:38:25.780 --> 00:38:28.540
one-by-one in every
environment, managing

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:32.110
security policy separately, and
having that fragmented state?

00:38:32.110 --> 00:38:33.770
That has people cost.

00:38:33.770 --> 00:38:35.480
It has platform cost.

00:38:35.480 --> 00:38:37.132
It adds risk.

00:38:37.132 --> 00:38:39.841
AUDIENCE: Do you have a use
case that you can share,

00:38:39.841 --> 00:38:45.109
where somebody was thinking
of moving or have moved?

00:38:45.109 --> 00:38:49.160
They're already in transition,
or they're ready to move.

00:38:49.160 --> 00:38:54.818
I had to make a TCO case, so OK,
this is where our [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:54.818 --> 00:38:56.982
or we can demonstrate
in the future that

00:38:56.982 --> 00:38:58.770
is going to be good.

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:01.385
APARNA SINHA: Yeah, so I mean,
we have several such customers.

00:39:01.385 --> 00:39:02.760
And they're not
necessarily using

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:04.150
all parts of the platform.

00:39:04.150 --> 00:39:07.170
But one that is-- and I don't
know that I can name them,

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:11.070
but it's a retailer, and
they're using GKE in the cloud.

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:16.040
And like I said, they
have almost 1,000 stores.

00:39:16.040 --> 00:39:19.113
And they want to be able
to run the same platform.

00:39:19.113 --> 00:39:20.780
And they've tried
many different things.

00:39:20.780 --> 00:39:23.330
They've tried a lot of
the existing technologies.

00:39:23.330 --> 00:39:26.360
But the multi-cluster management
capability, the ability

00:39:26.360 --> 00:39:29.620
to distribute workloads
from a single source,

00:39:29.620 --> 00:39:32.120
and to be able to manage policy
from a single source, that's

00:39:32.120 --> 00:39:32.750
very attractive.

00:39:32.750 --> 00:39:34.375
And that's what
they're-- they're like,

00:39:34.375 --> 00:39:35.400
I like GKE in the cloud.

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:36.410
It's fully managed.

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:38.127
I want that capability
in my stores.

00:39:38.127 --> 00:39:39.710
And if you give me
workload management

00:39:39.710 --> 00:39:41.498
on top that's awesome

00:39:41.498 --> 00:39:43.040
RAE WANG: And we
are actually working

00:39:43.040 --> 00:39:45.860
on a set of white papers
on TCO with example,

00:39:45.860 --> 00:39:49.358
so to quantify these values,
it's easier to tell your CIO.

00:39:49.358 --> 00:39:51.650
CHEN GOLDBERG: And specifically
talking about, I think,

00:39:51.650 --> 00:39:53.080
I know which customer.

00:39:53.080 --> 00:39:55.960
So they had to do it themselves.

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:59.270
So they wasted their
talent on a problem

00:39:59.270 --> 00:40:00.718
that we can solve for them.

00:40:00.718 --> 00:40:01.760
So that's actually clear.

00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:05.450
They say, OK, we're going to
get that to be more secured,

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:08.810
and we can innovate more.

00:40:08.810 --> 00:40:13.190
ALBERTO MAESTRO: The TCO, as
a preview of what is in there,

00:40:13.190 --> 00:40:14.420
you think about it.

00:40:14.420 --> 00:40:17.430
You have to run, deploy, and
manage your own Kubernetes

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:19.310
cluster is sort
of starting, then

00:40:19.310 --> 00:40:24.130
you have to start out in
Envoy proxies or Orchestration

00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:27.900
making sure that all
the upgrades go smooth.

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:32.590
If you start adding all
of the employee costs,

00:40:32.590 --> 00:40:35.890
time offline that you need.

00:40:35.890 --> 00:40:37.810
Most customers take
their cluster offline

00:40:37.810 --> 00:40:38.860
to upgrade them.

00:40:38.860 --> 00:40:40.510
So all of that starts adding up.

00:40:40.510 --> 00:40:43.380
And you will see that the
value proposition that we bring

00:40:43.380 --> 00:40:45.590
with Anthos is very compelling.

00:40:45.590 --> 00:40:47.980
That's in the short term,
just immediately, you

00:40:47.980 --> 00:40:49.120
can calculate it.

00:40:49.120 --> 00:40:52.690
There is an intangible
benefit that we're

00:40:52.690 --> 00:40:54.490
trying to drive
with Anthos, which

00:40:54.490 --> 00:40:56.020
is also the standardization.

00:40:56.020 --> 00:40:58.680
If you think about it, everybody
is running the Kubernetes

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:01.330
in a different way,
which means that it makes

00:41:01.330 --> 00:41:05.290
it impossible for
a service provider

00:41:05.290 --> 00:41:07.960
to deliver services onto
platform, because they

00:41:07.960 --> 00:41:09.310
don't know how to run it.

00:41:09.310 --> 00:41:12.610
They don't know which components
you're using, which versions,

00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:14.150
or how they've been configured.

00:41:14.150 --> 00:41:16.120
So an intangible
value of Anthos,

00:41:16.120 --> 00:41:18.183
it is a platform that is common.

00:41:18.183 --> 00:41:20.350
That means that it's going
to unlock the opportunity

00:41:20.350 --> 00:41:23.390
to have service providers
deliver the services they need,

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:25.065
as we were discussing
before, whether it

00:41:25.065 --> 00:41:26.360
be first party or third party.

00:41:26.360 --> 00:41:29.210
So there is the short term
total cost of ownership,

00:41:29.210 --> 00:41:31.060
but also,
strategically, you want

00:41:31.060 --> 00:41:32.800
to invest on a
platform that is going

00:41:32.800 --> 00:41:35.890
to have an ecosystem around it.

00:41:35.890 --> 00:41:39.970
JESS ELROY: One more small
thing, and we'll move on here.

00:41:39.970 --> 00:41:43.670
Part of that process that
you're talking about,

00:41:43.670 --> 00:41:45.510
one of those components
that I see a lot

00:41:45.510 --> 00:41:46.948
are security and compliance.

00:41:46.948 --> 00:41:49.240
And I'm thinking of a meeting
that a partner and I were

00:41:49.240 --> 00:41:53.680
in about a week ago, where
a large financial services

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:56.820
firm looking at that
type of TCO, there

00:41:56.820 --> 00:41:59.447
is the above-the-line TCO, where
you're going to save money.

00:41:59.447 --> 00:42:01.030
You're going to make
money, et cetera.

00:42:01.030 --> 00:42:02.613
But there is the
below-the-line, where

00:42:02.613 --> 00:42:06.700
you're going to save time and
all of that energy and effort.

00:42:06.700 --> 00:42:09.105
For every application
that you want to migrate,

00:42:09.105 --> 00:42:10.480
you've got to go
through, and you

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:13.370
have to justify the migration,
plan out the migration.

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:15.192
You've got to assess
all of the security,

00:42:15.192 --> 00:42:17.650
and then you have to assess
the compliance underneath that,

00:42:17.650 --> 00:42:18.150
too.

00:42:18.150 --> 00:42:20.410
Make sure that you're not
going to completely blow up

00:42:20.410 --> 00:42:22.120
your compliance state
when you migrate.

00:42:22.120 --> 00:42:26.110
So if we can standardize
all of that on Anthos,

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:29.390
then we can really streamline
a big chunk of that process

00:42:29.390 --> 00:42:30.342
for the customer.

00:42:33.200 --> 00:42:36.480
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so
regarding Anthos migration,

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:40.770
any plans for
bare-metal migration

00:42:40.770 --> 00:42:44.780
as part of moving the
data center to the cloud?

00:42:44.780 --> 00:42:47.720
APARNA SINHA: You mean,
migration to bare metal

00:42:47.720 --> 00:42:49.705
or migration from bare metal?

00:42:49.705 --> 00:42:50.330
AUDIENCE: From.

00:42:53.682 --> 00:42:55.140
APARNA SINHA: Right
now, we're very

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:57.870
focused on migrating
existing applications

00:42:57.870 --> 00:43:03.480
from virtual machines
to GKE, obviously,

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:07.500
to GCE, and to GKE On-Prem.

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:10.633
And so that's kind of
the roadmap for now.

00:43:10.633 --> 00:43:12.300
AUDIENCE: Because the
way I'm seeing it,

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:14.220
we have a lot of
legacy customers, who

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:19.410
are using all the legacy
data centers, which

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:25.200
has application developed
on real bare-metal servers.

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:28.950
Each server has its own
application and stuff

00:43:28.950 --> 00:43:31.485
like that, a lot
of customers who

00:43:31.485 --> 00:43:34.140
are not using virtual
machines and stuff like that.

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:36.870
And I believe that it
would be helpful for them

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:38.550
if you would be
able to provide them

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:41.730
with an agent that would be able
to migrate them into Anthos.

00:43:41.730 --> 00:43:44.490
That would be, as
far as I see it,

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:47.430
a valid use case to move
to Kubernetes and stuff

00:43:47.430 --> 00:43:50.110
like that easily.

00:43:50.110 --> 00:43:52.430
So that's it.

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:52.930
Thank you.

00:43:52.930 --> 00:43:53.800
APARNA SINHA: That's
good feedback.

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:54.300
Thank you.

00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:02.320
AUDIENCE: I think I want to take
this a little bit different.

00:44:02.320 --> 00:44:05.300
Part of this vision for
Anthos, and the service mesh,

00:44:05.300 --> 00:44:07.970
and bringing in the Marketplace,
really, it seems like,

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:11.140
relies on identity
and access management.

00:44:11.140 --> 00:44:13.220
I really want to hear
more about what Google

00:44:13.220 --> 00:44:17.120
is doing to plug in our existing
identities in access management

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:21.843
environments and systems
into the service mesh.

00:44:21.843 --> 00:44:24.260
I don't want to have to reinvent
it for Anthos, because we

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:26.690
already have it.

00:44:26.690 --> 00:44:28.880
We are also G Suite
school, so we also

00:44:28.880 --> 00:44:30.200
have it in Google already.

00:44:33.090 --> 00:44:35.510
RAE WANG: So there's identity
and access management,

00:44:35.510 --> 00:44:37.400
kind of talk about
both of them, right?

00:44:37.400 --> 00:44:41.990
In terms of identity, some
of the most important changes

00:44:41.990 --> 00:44:44.240
coming from service
mesh is, in the past,

00:44:44.240 --> 00:44:45.920
we all used service accounts.

00:44:45.920 --> 00:44:49.710
You copy key, put it in GitHub,
and all hell breaks loose.

00:44:49.710 --> 00:44:51.560
And then, really,
service identity

00:44:51.560 --> 00:44:56.070
is as we see the future of
the identity for services.

00:44:56.070 --> 00:44:58.968
So we are more and more
looking at best practices

00:44:58.968 --> 00:45:01.010
based on service identities
and more and more are

00:45:01.010 --> 00:45:03.500
going to try to hide service
account as an implementation

00:45:03.500 --> 00:45:04.250
detail.

00:45:04.250 --> 00:45:07.460
Really think about it as a
strong identity of services

00:45:07.460 --> 00:45:10.670
and try to make you do
as little of the copying

00:45:10.670 --> 00:45:11.800
keys as possible.

00:45:11.800 --> 00:45:13.708
Eventually, you should
forget about keys.

00:45:13.708 --> 00:45:15.500
A strong way to
authenticate your services,

00:45:15.500 --> 00:45:19.100
and that's a much safer way
to express your policies.

00:45:19.100 --> 00:45:23.400
And then in terms
of user identity,

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:25.730
Anthos allows us to think
about, not only your impasse

00:45:25.730 --> 00:45:27.320
is showing employee's identity.

00:45:27.320 --> 00:45:29.570
You use Cloud Identity,
you do a federation, so on.

00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:31.190
But also, with GKE
On-Prem, now, we're

00:45:31.190 --> 00:45:34.970
building connections,
integrations

00:45:34.970 --> 00:45:40.640
with your existing on-prem
IDP, such as Active Directory.

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:42.500
But we're also now
thinking about you

00:45:42.500 --> 00:45:43.990
serving your customers.

00:45:43.990 --> 00:45:46.900
So the new identity
platform we're launching

00:45:46.900 --> 00:45:50.330
is about, how do you
provide an identity service

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:51.910
for your applications'
customers,

00:45:51.910 --> 00:45:54.270
kind of extending beyond
just your developers,

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:56.900
but to your customers as well.

00:45:56.900 --> 00:46:01.550
And then in terms of
access management,

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:04.940
GCP has its own IM
policies, native policy

00:46:04.940 --> 00:46:06.050
for the infrastructures.

00:46:06.050 --> 00:46:09.170
And then with
Kubernetes and Istio,

00:46:09.170 --> 00:46:12.320
they also come with the
Kubernetes-style API and RBAC

00:46:12.320 --> 00:46:15.600
and the Kubernetes
style of policies.

00:46:15.600 --> 00:46:19.040
Right now, I think, with
CSP config management,

00:46:19.040 --> 00:46:20.870
we help you distribute
those consistently

00:46:20.870 --> 00:46:23.630
across other
clusters and meshes.

00:46:23.630 --> 00:46:27.140
We are kind of looking to
what would it take for--

00:46:27.140 --> 00:46:29.300
this is a common problem, right?

00:46:29.300 --> 00:46:31.640
So today, we have it
between the new Kubernetes

00:46:31.640 --> 00:46:32.887
system and GCP IM.

00:46:32.887 --> 00:46:34.220
But you also have that with SQL.

00:46:34.220 --> 00:46:35.653
You also have that with Linux.

00:46:35.653 --> 00:46:37.070
One example we've
done in the past

00:46:37.070 --> 00:46:40.550
is we've created a bridge
between your GCP IM

00:46:40.550 --> 00:46:42.990
system and the Linux
permission system.

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:44.760
So you have this
user log-in feature.

00:46:44.760 --> 00:46:46.760
We're looking at additional
opportunities, where

00:46:46.760 --> 00:46:49.910
you have ping points managing
across multiple of these IM

00:46:49.910 --> 00:46:50.690
systems.

00:46:50.690 --> 00:46:52.880
We are looking how to build
those bridges for you.

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:54.650
APARNA SINHA: Anthos
is really optimized

00:46:54.650 --> 00:46:57.380
for hybrid and multi-cloud.

00:46:57.380 --> 00:47:01.850
And so the configuration and
policy management in Anthos

00:47:01.850 --> 00:47:07.270
is very much based on Kubernetes
RBAC and quota management,

00:47:07.270 --> 00:47:10.220
and Istio configuration,
and managing that

00:47:10.220 --> 00:47:12.530
from a centralized
source of truth.

00:47:12.530 --> 00:47:15.010
I think the question,
perhaps, is,

00:47:15.010 --> 00:47:18.950
can that centralized source
of truth be IM, GCP IM,

00:47:18.950 --> 00:47:19.460
for example?

00:47:24.923 --> 00:47:26.340
AUDIENCE: Can that
source of truth

00:47:26.340 --> 00:47:30.290
be the IM system
that we already have?

00:47:30.290 --> 00:47:32.390
Tying in the GCP IM--

00:47:32.390 --> 00:47:35.140
great, but I really don't
want three different systems.

00:47:35.140 --> 00:47:36.470
APARNA SINHA: So, certainly.

00:47:36.470 --> 00:47:41.630
As Rae mentioned, we are
doing identity federation

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:44.090
with active directory
through Google Cloud.

00:47:44.090 --> 00:47:45.720
So yes.

00:47:45.720 --> 00:47:47.660
RAE WANG: And we're
looking for ways to--

00:47:47.660 --> 00:47:49.210
through Kubernetes
Istio, we learned,

00:47:49.210 --> 00:47:52.250
the only way to really have
consistency across multi-cloud

00:47:52.250 --> 00:47:55.730
is to have it attached
to an open standard.

00:47:55.730 --> 00:47:58.460
So we're looking at, what are
the abstract concepts that

00:47:58.460 --> 00:48:02.000
are not provider-specific
that we can leverage?

00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:04.233
For example, namespace
is a great concept.

00:48:04.233 --> 00:48:05.400
Service identity is another.

00:48:05.400 --> 00:48:09.560
So we're looking at how
to evolve both GCP IM,

00:48:09.560 --> 00:48:12.740
as well as RBAC policies,
with the community

00:48:12.740 --> 00:48:16.520
towards an
abstraction layer that

00:48:16.520 --> 00:48:19.528
can live on top of multiple
different platforms.

00:48:22.343 --> 00:48:23.760
AUDIENCE: How will
it be licensed?

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:25.540
How am I going to buy this?

00:48:25.540 --> 00:48:26.824
RAE WANG: Great question.

00:48:26.824 --> 00:48:28.460
You want to speak to that.

00:48:28.460 --> 00:48:31.820
APARNA SINHA: Yeah,
so the licensing,

00:48:31.820 --> 00:48:34.580
it's a subscription
license that is

00:48:34.580 --> 00:48:40.970
based on a per vCPU managed
by the platform as calculated

00:48:40.970 --> 00:48:43.800
by Kubernetes metering.

00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:47.840
So that's the model, and
it's in blocks of 100 vCPUs.

00:48:47.840 --> 00:48:51.710
So we expect users will want to
purchase several blocks of 100

00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:57.080
vCPUs billed monthly,
but signing up

00:48:57.080 --> 00:49:00.255
for a subscription
for, say, a year.

00:49:00.255 --> 00:49:01.130
MODERATOR: Thank you.

00:49:01.130 --> 00:49:02.550
We're almost at time.

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:04.670
And I wonder if each of
the panelists would say--

00:49:04.670 --> 00:49:05.670
oh, you have a question.

00:49:05.670 --> 00:49:07.150
OK, last question.

00:49:09.900 --> 00:49:13.110
AUDIENCE: Since this is
an AMA, I have to ask,

00:49:13.110 --> 00:49:16.822
one horse-sized duck or
100 duck-sized horses--

00:49:16.822 --> 00:49:18.030
which would you rather fight?

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:24.430
